# heat-engine, a slice: wet-bulb temperature, heat flags, the canonical operation, and the
# request rules they need. Adapted from github.com/craigm26/regen-heat-engine at 4d99222
# (.regenerate/SPEC.md 1.0.2 and DECISIONS.md; MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 craigm26).
# Slice versions: 1.0.2-slice was the duramen 0.1 restatement; 1.1.0-slice adds evidence,
# properties, result types and static checks (duramen diff: tightening, so a new minor).
# Left out of the slice: work/rest, verdict and the weather cascade. REGEN.json and the budgets
# are in, as static checks on the implementation folder (section BU).
# The oracle is that repository's suite oracle, unchanged, behind a small driver. Two evidence
# files, evidence/*.csv, are unchanged copies of HeatCompass/heat-engine-spec's fixtures at
# f621520 (MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 HeatCompass), as imported by regen-heat-engine.

duramen 0.2

spec heat-engine 1.1.0-slice
  title "Wet-bulb temperature and heat flags, with byte-exact audit records"
  contract 0.2.0
  request {"clock": "2026-05-26T17:00:00.000Z"}
  text
    `heat-engine` is a small calculation library for outdoor heat-stress decisions. This slice
    covers wet-bulb temperature from air temperature and relative humidity (Stull 2011), from
    °C or °F, and the U.S. Marine Corps heat flag (MCO 6200.1E) for a wet-bulb temperature in °F
    or °C. Every computing operation returns its result together with an **audit record**: what
    was computed, from which inputs and constants, under which citation, and when. Audit
    records are compared byte for byte, so their serialized form is part of the contract.
    "Number" means an IEEE 754 binary64 value (a JavaScript `number`, a Python `float`).

oracle node oracle-driver.mjs
  source oracle.mjs

type num = number | "NaN" | "Infinity" | "-Infinity"
type wetBulbResult = {wetBulbC: number, wetBulbF: number, clampedRhPct?: number} | null
type flag = "white" | "green" | "yellow" | "red" | "black"
type flagResult = {flag: flag, flagDartLabel: "low" | "moderate" | "high" | "extreme" | "critical"} | null
type command = string | {default: string, ...}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
section RQ "Requests"

errors
  bad_request when the line is not a JSON object, or `id` is missing or not a string (the
    response then has `"id": null`)
  unknown_op when `op` is missing, not a string, or not one of the operations
  bad_request when `input` is missing or not an object; `clock` is missing or not a string
    where the operation needs it; a required input field is missing; or an input field has the
    wrong JSON type (a number field holding `true` or `"80"`, for example)

req IF-004 "The injected clock"
  text
    Every request except `canonical` carries `clock`, a UTC timestamp in the exact form
    `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS.sssZ` (24 characters). It is the injected clock: every `computed_at`
    in every audit record produced by the request MUST equal it exactly. An implementation
    never reads the wall clock for an audit.
  example flagF {"wetBulbF": 70}
    request {"clock": "1999-12-31T23:59:59.999Z"}
    expect audit.computed_at = "1999-12-31T23:59:59.999Z"
  example flagC {"wetBulbC": 30}
    request {"clock": "1999-12-31T23:59:59.999Z"}
    expect audit.children.0.computed_at = "1999-12-31T23:59:59.999Z"

req IF-006 "Numbers in requests"
  decision D-007
  text
    Numeric input fields accept either a JSON number or one of the three strings `"NaN"`,
    `"Infinity"`, `"-Infinity"`, which stand for the corresponding non-finite values. This is
    how invalid numeric input reaches the operations. A JSON number in a request MUST be
    treated as a binary64 value regardless of how it is written: `20`, `20.0` and `2e1` are
    the same input and are written identically (`20`).
  example wetBulb {"tempC": 2.0e1, "rhPercent": 50.000}
    expect audit.result_summary = "T=20.0°C RH=50% → Tw=13.70°C"
  example flagC {"wetBulbC": "-Infinity"}
    expect result = null
    expect audit.inputs = {"wetBulbC": "-Infinity"}

req IF-007 "Requests that cannot be handled"
  decision D-019, D-024
  text
    A request line that cannot be handled MUST get exactly `{"id": <id>, "error": <code>}`,
    with the code given by the numbered list under Errors, and the driver MUST then continue
    with the next line. Invalid values are not errors: a non-finite number or an unknown flag
    name reaches the operation, which reports it in its result.
  example raw "{not json"
    expect id = null
    expect error = "bad_request"
  example raw "[1,2]"
    expect id = null
    expect error = "bad_request"
  example flagF {"wetBulbF": 80}
    omit id
    expect id = null
    expect error = "bad_request"
  example heatIndex {"tempC": 20}
    expect error = "unknown_op"
  example flagF {"wetBulbF": 80}
    omit op
    expect error = "unknown_op"
  example heatIndex
    omit clock
    expect error = "unknown_op"
  example flagF
    expect error = "bad_request"
  example flagF {"wetBulbF": 80}
    omit clock
    expect error = "bad_request"
  example wetBulb {"tempC": 20}
    expect error = "bad_request"
  example flagF {"wetBulbF": true}
    expect error = "bad_request"
  example flagF {"wetBulbF": "80"}
    expect error = "bad_request"

req IF-008 "Extra members"
  text
    Unknown extra members in a request or in `input` MUST be ignored.
  example raw '{"id":"extra","op":"flagF","input":{"wetBulbF":86,"note":"x"},"clock":"2026-05-26T17:00:00.000Z","trace":true}'
    expect result = {"flag": "yellow", "flagDartLabel": "high"}

open IF-001 "Standard error"
  text
    What the driver writes to standard error.

open IF-002 "Error text"
  text
    Any human-readable error text; it never appears in a response.

open IF-003 "Malformed clocks"
  text
    Behavior for a `clock` string that is not in the exact 24-character form.

open IF-005 "Streaming and odd request bytes"
  text
    Whether responses are written as each request line arrives or only after end of input
    (only order and completeness are pinned); handling of request bytes that are not valid
    UTF-8; handling of duplicate member names in a request object.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
section CJ "Canonical JSON"
  text
    Audit records, result summaries and the `canonical` operation write numbers and JSON by the
    edges in the Edges section: number text (number-text/ecmascript), fixed-point text
    (fixed-text/ecmascript) and canonical JSON (json/sorted-utf16).

edge number-text/ecmascript via canonical value
  decision D-002
edge fixed-text/ecmascript
  decision D-002
edge json/sorted-utf16 via canonical value
  decision D-021

op canonical
  input value any
  returns string
  result the canonical JSON text of `value`, as a string
  request {}

req CJ-005 "The canonical operation"
  decision D-017
  text
    `canonical` takes `input.value`, any JSON value, `null` included, and MUST respond
    `{"id": <id>, "result": <string>}`, where the string is the canonical JSON text of the
    value (edge json/sorted-utf16). It has no `audit` member and needs no `clock`. JSON numbers
    in `value` are binary64 values (REQ-IF-006); the special strings of REQ-IF-006 are **not**
    interpreted here: they are ordinary strings. Without a `value` member the request is a
    `bad_request`.
  example canonical {"value": {"b": [1, 2.50, 1e21], "a": null}}
    expect result = "{\"a\":null,\"b\":[1,2.5,1e+21]}"
  example canonical {"value": {"y": "Infinity", "x": "NaN"}}
    expect result = "{\"x\":\"NaN\",\"y\":\"Infinity\"}"
  example canonical {"value": null}
    expect result = "null"
  example canonical {}
    expect error = "bad_request"

property CJ-P1 "Canonical text is a fixed point and reads back as the value"
  supports CJ-005
  for v in json
  call a = canonical {"value": v}
  call b = canonical {"value": parse(a.result)}
  expect b.result == a.result
  expect parse(a.result) == v

open CJ-001 "Numbers outside binary64"
  text
    JSON numbers outside the binary64 range (e.g. `1e400`), anywhere in a request: their
    canonical text in `canonical`, and how operations treat them.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
section AU "Audit records"

req AU-001 "Audit members"
  decision D-001
  text
    Every audit record is a JSON object with these members, always present:

    | member | value |
    |---|---|
    | `spec_version` | the string `"0.2.0"` |
    | `function` | the operation's function name (given per operation) |
    | `inputs` | an object of the inputs, as each operation specifies |
    | `constants` | an object mapping constant names to numbers, as each operation specifies (possibly empty) |
    | `citation` | a fixed string per operation |
    | `result_summary` | a one-line human-readable string, as each operation specifies |
    | `computed_at` | the request's `clock` (REQ-IF-004) |

    `flagC` adds `children` (REQ-FL-005). No other members appear. In a response, `audit` is
    this record written as canonical JSON (edge json/sorted-utf16) and carried as a JSON string.
  decision D-003
  example flagF {"wetBulbF": 85}
    expect audit.spec_version = "0.2.0"
    expect audit.function = "flagFromWetBulbF"
    expect audit.computed_at = "2026-05-26T17:00:00.000Z"

property AU-P1 "Audits are canonical JSON text"
  supports AU-001
  for t in -20 .. 50
  for rh in 0 .. 120
  call a = wetBulb {"tempC": t, "rhPercent": rh}
  call b = flagC {"wetBulbC": a.result.wetBulbC}
  call ca = canonical {"value": parse(a.audit)}
  call cb = canonical {"value": parse(b.audit)}
  expect ca.result == a.audit
  expect cb.result == b.audit

req AU-002 "Non-finite inputs in audits"
  decision D-007
  text
    In `inputs`, a non-finite number MUST be written as the string `"NaN"`, `"Infinity"` or
    `"-Infinity"` (not as `null`).
  example wetBulb {"tempC": "Infinity", "rhPercent": "NaN"}
    expect audit.inputs = {"tempC": "Infinity", "rhPercent": "NaN"}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
section WB "Wet-bulb temperature (Stull 2011)"
  text
    Primary source: Stull, R. (2011), *Wet-Bulb Temperature from Relative Humidity and Air
    Temperature*, J. Appl. Meteor. Climatol. 50(11):2267–2269, DOI 10.1175/JAMC-D-11-0143.1.

op wetBulb
  input tempC num, rhPercent num
  returns wetBulbResult
  result `wetBulbC`, `wetBulbF`, and `clampedRhPct` when RH was clamped (REQ-WB-001)
  tolerance result.wetBulbC 0.00001
  tolerance result.wetBulbF 0.000018001
  audit text

req WB-001 "Wet-bulb temperature"
  decision D-009, D-018
  text
    For finite `tempC` (T, °C) and `rhPercent`, let `RH` be `rhPercent` clamped to the closed
    range [5, 100] (below 5 becomes 5, above 100 becomes 100). Compute, in binary64,
    evaluating exactly these terms in this order:

    ```
    term1 = T * atan(0.151977 * sqrt(RH + 8.313659))
    term2 = atan(T + RH)
    term3 = atan(RH - 1.676331)
    term4 = 0.00391838 * RH^1.5 * atan(0.023101 * RH)
    wetBulbC = term1 + term2 - term3 + term4 + (-4.686035)
    wetBulbF = (wetBulbC * 9) / 5 + 32
    ```

    `RH^1.5` is the library power function with exponent 1.5. The result MUST be
    `{"wetBulbC": wetBulbC, "wetBulbF": wetBulbF}`, plus `"clampedRhPct": RH` only when
    `RH ≠ rhPercent`.
  table wetBulb
    | tempC | rhPercent | result.wetBulbC ± 0.00001 | result.clampedRhPct |
    | 20    | 50        | 13.69934                  |                     |
    | 25    | 120       | 25.04558                  | 100                 |
    | 30    | 2.5       | 10.77218                  | 5                   |
    | 25    | 99.5      | 24.97823                  |                     |

req WB-002 "No clamping of temperature"
  text
    Temperatures outside [-20, 50] °C still compute a result: `tempC` is never clamped.
  table wetBulb
    | tempC | rhPercent | result.wetBulbC ± 0.00001 |
    | 60    | 50        | 48.08736                  |
    | -30   | 50        | -29.31486                 |

req WB-003 "Wet-bulb audit record"
  decision D-001
  text
    The audit record for a finite computation:

    - `function`: `"calculateWetBulb"`; `citation`: `"Stull (2011) eq. 1"`.
    - `inputs`: `{"tempC": tempC, "rhPercent": rhPercent}`, with the values as given (before
      clamping).
    - `constants`: `stull_a` 0.151977, `stull_b` 8.313659, `stull_c` 1.676331, `stull_d`
      0.00391838, `stull_e` 0.023101, `stull_offset` -4.686035; when RH was clamped, also
      `rh_clamp_min` 5 and `rh_clamp_max` 100.
    - `result_summary`: `T=<T>°C RH=<rh><markers> → Tw=<Tw>°C`, where
      - `<T>` is `tempC` fixed to 1 place (edge fixed-text/ecmascript);
      - `<rh>` is `<RH>%` when not clamped, or `<rhPercent>→<RH>%` when clamped, both as
        number text (edge number-text/ecmascript);
      - `<markers>` is empty, or ` (` + a comma-separated list + `)`: `rh_clamped` when
        clamped, then `out_of_validity_range` when `tempC < -20` or `tempC > 50`;
      - `<Tw>` is `wetBulbC` fixed to 2 places.
  example wetBulb {"tempC": 25, "rhPercent": 120}
    expect audit.inputs = {"tempC": 25, "rhPercent": 120}
    expect audit.constants = {"stull_a": 0.151977, "stull_b": 8.313659, "stull_c": 1.676331, "stull_d": 0.00391838, "stull_e": 0.023101, "stull_offset": -4.686035, "rh_clamp_min": 5, "rh_clamp_max": 100}
  table wetBulb
    | tempC | rhPercent | audit.result_summary                                                 |
    | 20    | 50        | "T=20.0°C RH=50% → Tw=13.70°C"                                       |
    | 25    | 120       | "T=25.0°C RH=120→100% (rh_clamped) → Tw=25.05°C"                     |
    | 60    | 2.5       | "T=60.0°C RH=2.5→5% (rh_clamped,out_of_validity_range) → Tw=25.97°C" |
    | 20.25 | 50        | "T=20.3°C RH=50% → Tw=13.91°C"                                       |
    | -0.04 | 50        | "T=-0.0°C RH=50% → Tw=-3.53°C"                                       |

req WB-004 "Non-finite wet-bulb input"
  decision D-007, D-019
  text
    If `tempC` or `rhPercent` is not finite, the result MUST be `null` and the audit MUST have
    `function` and `citation` as above, `inputs` with both values (REQ-AU-002), `constants`
    `{}`, and `result_summary` `invalid_input:tempC` if `tempC` is not finite, otherwise
    `invalid_input:rhPercent`.
  table wetBulb
    | tempC      | rhPercent   | result | audit.result_summary      |
    | "NaN"      | 50          | null   | "invalid_input:tempC"     |
    | 20         | "NaN"       | null   | "invalid_input:rhPercent" |
    | "Infinity" | 50          | null   | "invalid_input:tempC"     |
    | 20         | "-Infinity" | null   | "invalid_input:rhPercent" |
    | "NaN"      | "NaN"       | null   | "invalid_input:tempC"     |

op wetBulbF
  input tempF num, rhPercent num
  returns wetBulbResult
  result as `wetBulb` (REQ-WB-005)
  tolerance result.wetBulbC 0.00001
  tolerance result.wetBulbF 0.000018001
  audit text

req WB-005 "Wet-bulb from °F"
  decision D-010
  text
    `wetBulbF` takes `tempF` and `rhPercent`, computes `tempC = ((tempF - 32) * 5) / 9` in
    binary64 (this exact expression), and then MUST behave exactly as `wetBulb` with that
    `tempC` and the same `rhPercent`. The result and the audit are those of `wetBulb`
    (`function` is `"calculateWetBulb"`; `inputs` holds the converted `tempC`). A non-finite
    `tempF` gives a non-finite `tempC` and so `invalid_input:tempC`.
  example wetBulbF {"tempF": 68, "rhPercent": 50}
    expect audit.result_summary = "T=20.0°C RH=50% → Tw=13.70°C"
    expect audit.inputs = {"tempC": 20, "rhPercent": 50}
  example wetBulbF {"tempF": 100, "rhPercent": 40}
    expect audit.inputs = {"tempC": 37.77777777777778, "rhPercent": 40}
  example wetBulbF {"tempF": 98.6, "rhPercent": 50}
    expect audit.inputs = {"tempC": 37, "rhPercent": 50}
  example wetBulbF {"tempF": "NaN", "rhPercent": 50}
    expect result = null
    expect audit.result_summary = "invalid_input:tempC"

evidence WB-FIXTURES "Wet-bulb values from an independent program"
  source HeatCompass/heat-engine-spec at f621520, spec/tier1-foundation/wet-bulb.fixtures.csv, written by its scripts/build-fixtures.py (Python, not this spec's oracle)
  kind computed
  supports WB-001
  table wetBulb from "evidence/wet-bulb.fixtures.csv"
    id id
    columns tempC = temp_c, rhPercent = rh_percent
    expect result.wetBulbC ≈ expected_wet_bulb_c ± tolerance_c
    expect result.wetBulbF ≈ expected_wet_bulb_f ± 0.000018001

evidence WB-EXTENDED "Wet-bulb values outside Stull's range, from the same independent formula"
  source evidence/make-extended.py, which runs stull() copied unchanged from HeatCompass/heat-engine-spec's scripts/build-fixtures.py at f621520 (Python, not this spec's oracle)
  kind computed
  supports WB-002
  text
    Added after `duramen mutate` showed that a change in the sixth decimal place of one of
    the formula's constants went unnoticed: inside Stull's range it moves results by less than
    the tolerance, and outside it nothing independent checked the values.
  table wetBulb from "evidence/wet-bulb-extended.csv"
    id id
    columns tempC = temp_c, rhPercent = rh_percent
    expect result.wetBulbC ≈ expected_wet_bulb_c ± tolerance_c
    expect result.wetBulbF ≈ expected_wet_bulb_f ± 0.000018001

property WB-P8 "RH is clamped exactly when it is outside [5, 100]"
  supports WB-001
  decision D-009
  samples 40
  for t in -20 .. 50
  for rh in one of 4.999999, 5, 5.000001, 5.5, 6, 50, 99.999999, 100, 100.000001, 101
  call a = wetBulb {"tempC": t, "rhPercent": rh}
  expect has(a.result, "clampedRhPct") == (rh < 5 or rh > 100)

property WB-P1 "wetBulbF is wetBulb on the converted temperature"
  supports WB-005
  decision D-010
  for f in -100 .. 200
  for rh in 0 .. 150
  call a = wetBulbF {"tempF": f, "rhPercent": rh}
  call b = wetBulb {"tempC": (f - 32) * 5 / 9, "rhPercent": rh}
  expect a.result == b.result
  expect a.audit == b.audit

property WB-P2 "Humidity above 100 computes as 100"
  supports WB-001
  decision D-009
  for t in -20 .. 50
  for rh in 100.001 .. 1000
  call a = wetBulb {"tempC": t, "rhPercent": rh}
  call b = wetBulb {"tempC": t, "rhPercent": 100}
  expect a.result.wetBulbC == b.result.wetBulbC and a.result.wetBulbF == b.result.wetBulbF
  expect a.result.clampedRhPct == 100 and not has(b.result, "clampedRhPct")

property WB-P3 "Humidity below 5 computes as 5"
  supports WB-001
  decision D-009
  for t in -20 .. 50
  for rh in -100 .. 4.999
  call a = wetBulb {"tempC": t, "rhPercent": rh}
  call b = wetBulb {"tempC": t, "rhPercent": 5}
  expect a.result.wetBulbC == b.result.wetBulbC and a.result.clampedRhPct == 5

property WB-P4 "wetBulbF is the °F of wetBulbC"
  supports WB-001
  for t in -60 .. 60
  for rh in 0 .. 120
  call a = wetBulb {"tempC": t, "rhPercent": rh}
  expect a.result.wetBulbF == (a.result.wetBulbC * 9) / 5 + 32

property WB-P5 "From 5 °C up, more humidity gives a higher wet-bulb"
  supports WB-001
  text
    Stull's fit is not monotonic everywhere in its range: up to about 3 °C, more humidity can
    give a lower result (at -20 °C, going from 5 % to 6 % RH lowers it by 0.37 °C). This
    specification pins the formula, so that behavior is part of it; the property is stated
    where the formula rises.
  for t in 5 .. 50
  for rh in 5 .. 99
  call a = wetBulb {"tempC": t, "rhPercent": rh}
  call b = wetBulb {"tempC": t, "rhPercent": rh + 1}
  expect b.result.wetBulbC > a.result.wetBulbC

property WB-P7 "The validity marker, at and around -20 and 50"
  supports WB-003
  for t in one of -20, 50, -20.000001, 50.000001, -19.999999, 49.999999, -60, 60, 0
  for rh in 5 .. 100
  call a = wetBulb {"tempC": t, "rhPercent": rh}
  expect contains(parse(a.audit).result_summary, "out_of_validity_range") == (t < -20 or t > 50)

property WB-P6 "The audit records the inputs as given"
  supports WB-003
  for t in -60 .. 60
  for rh in 0 .. 120
  call a = wetBulb {"tempC": t, "rhPercent": rh}
  expect parse(a.audit).inputs == {"tempC": t, "rhPercent": rh}

open WB-001 "Overflow"
  text
    Results when the computation overflows or loses all precision (inputs of enormous
    magnitude); the result and summary for such inputs are unspecified.

open WB-002 "Extra precision"
  text
    Results more precise than the stated tolerance.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
section FL "Heat flags (MCO 6200.1E)"
  text
    Primary source: U.S. Marine Corps Order 6200.1E, Marine Corps Heat Stress Program.

op flagF
  input wetBulbF num
  returns flagResult
  result `flag` and `flagDartLabel` (REQ-FL-001)
  audit text

req FL-001 "Flag from °F"
  decision D-010
  text
    For finite `wetBulbF` (`w` below), the result MUST be
    `{"flag": <flag>, "flagDartLabel": <label>}` from this table. The bands are in °F with
    inclusive lower bounds, and comparisons are on the binary64 value as given.

    | flag | wet-bulb °F | label |
    |---|---|---|
    | `white` | `w < 80` | `low` |
    | `green` | `80 ≤ w < 85` | `moderate` |
    | `yellow` | `85 ≤ w < 88` | `high` |
    | `red` | `88 ≤ w < 90` | `extreme` |
    | `black` | `w ≥ 90` | `critical` |
  table flagF
    | wetBulbF | result.flag | result.flagDartLabel |
    | 79.99    | "white"     | "low"                |
    | 80       | "green"     | "moderate"           |
    | 84.99    | "green"     | "moderate"           |
    | 85       | "yellow"    | "high"               |
    | 88       | "red"       | "extreme"            |
    | 89.99    | "red"       | "extreme"            |
    | 90       | "black"     | "critical"           |

req FL-002 "Flag audit record"
  decision D-001
  text
    The audit: `function` `"flagFromWetBulbF"`; `citation` `"USMC 6200.1E Table 3-1"`;
    `inputs` `{"wetBulbF": wetBulbF}`; `constants`
    `{"white_max":80,"green_max":85,"yellow_max":88,"red_max":90}`; `result_summary`
    `wetBulbF=<w> → <flag>` with `<w>` as number text, followed by ` (out_of_observed_range)`
    when `wetBulbF < -50` or `wetBulbF > 200`.
  example flagF {"wetBulbF": 86.5}
    expect audit.constants = {"white_max": 80, "green_max": 85, "yellow_max": 88, "red_max": 90}
    expect audit.inputs = {"wetBulbF": 86.5}
  table flagF
    | wetBulbF | audit.result_summary                             |
    | 85       | "wetBulbF=85 → yellow"                           |
    | 250      | "wetBulbF=250 → black (out_of_observed_range)"   |
    | -60      | "wetBulbF=-60 → white (out_of_observed_range)"   |
    | 200      | "wetBulbF=200 → black"                           |

req FL-003 "Non-finite °F"
  decision D-007
  text
    For non-finite `wetBulbF` the result MUST be `null`; the audit has the same `function` and
    `citation`, `inputs` per REQ-AU-002, `constants` `{}`, and `result_summary`
    `invalid_input:wetBulbF`.
  example flagF {"wetBulbF": "NaN"}
    expect result = null
    expect audit.result_summary = "invalid_input:wetBulbF"
    expect audit.constants = {}

op flagC
  input wetBulbC num
  returns flagResult
  result as `flagF` (REQ-FL-004)
  audit text

req FL-004 "Flag from °C"
  decision D-010
  text
    For finite `wetBulbC`, compute `wetBulbF = (wetBulbC * 9) / 5 + 32` in binary64, with
    exactly this expression (not `wetBulbC * 1.8 + 32`, which rounds differently near the
    boundaries), then classify as `flagF`. The result MUST be the `flagF` result.
  table flagC
    | wetBulbC           | result.flag |
    | 26.66666666666666  | "white"     |
    | 30                 | "yellow"    |
    | 29.444444444444443 | "yellow"    |
    | 29.444444443444443 | "green"     |

req FL-005 "°C flag audit record"
  decision D-001
  text
    The audit: `function` `"flagFromWetBulbC"`; `citation` `"USMC 6200.1E Table 3-1"`;
    `inputs` `{"wetBulbC": wetBulbC}`; the same four `constants` as `flagF`; `result_summary`
    `wetBulbC=<c> → wetBulbF=<f> → <flag>`, with `<c>` as number text and `<f>` fixed to 4
    places; and `children`: an array of one element, the complete `flagF` audit record for the
    converted value.
  example flagC {"wetBulbC": 30}
    expect audit.result_summary = "wetBulbC=30 → wetBulbF=86.0000 → yellow"
    expect audit.children.0.result_summary = "wetBulbF=86 → yellow"
  example flagC {"wetBulbC": 26.66666666666666}
    expect audit.result_summary = "wetBulbC=26.66666666666666 → wetBulbF=80.0000 → white"

req FL-006 "Non-finite °C"
  decision D-007
  text
    For non-finite `wetBulbC` the result MUST be `null`; the audit has `function`
    `"flagFromWetBulbC"`, the same `citation`, `inputs` per REQ-AU-002, `constants` `{}`,
    `result_summary` `invalid_input:wetBulbC`, and no `children`.
  example flagC {"wetBulbC": "NaN"}
    expect result = null
    expect audit.result_summary = "invalid_input:wetBulbC"

evidence FL-FIXTURES "Flag rows worked out from MCO 6200.1E's boundaries"
  source HeatCompass/heat-engine-spec at f621520, spec/tier1-foundation/flag-mapping.fixtures.csv
  kind derived
  supports FL-001
  table flagF from "evidence/flag-mapping.fixtures.csv"
    id id
    columns wetBulbF = wet_bulb_f
    expect result.flag = expected_flag as string
    expect result.flagDartLabel = expected_flag_dart_label as string

property FL-P1 "flagC is flagF of the converted temperature"
  supports FL-004, FL-005
  decision D-010
  for c in -50 .. 60
  call a = flagC {"wetBulbC": c}
  call b = flagF {"wetBulbF": (c * 9) / 5 + 32}
  expect a.result == b.result
  expect parse(a.audit).children == [parse(b.audit)]

property FL-P4 "The observed-range marker, at and around -50 and 200"
  supports FL-002
  samples 30
  for w in one of -50, -50.000001, -49.999999, -50.5, 200, 200.000001, 199.999999, 200.5, 0
  call a = flagF {"wetBulbF": w}
  expect contains(parse(a.audit).result_summary, "out_of_observed_range") == (w < -50 or w > 200)

property FL-P2 "Each flag has one label"
  supports FL-001
  for w in -60 .. 250
  call a = flagF {"wetBulbF": w}
  expect {"white": "low", "green": "moderate", "yellow": "high", "red": "extreme", "black": "critical"}[a.result.flag] == a.result.flagDartLabel

property FL-P3 "A hotter wet-bulb never gives a milder flag"
  supports FL-001
  for w in -60 .. 250
  for dw in 0 .. 15
  call a = flagF {"wetBulbF": w}
  call b = flagF {"wetBulbF": w + dw}
  expect {"white": 0, "green": 1, "yellow": 2, "red": 3, "black": 4}[b.result.flag] >= {"white": 0, "green": 1, "yellow": 2, "red": 3, "black": 4}[a.result.flag]

open FL-001 "Overflow in the °C path"
  text
    `flagC` for a finite `wetBulbC` whose °F conversion overflows to a non-finite value (only
    reachable near ±1.8e308).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
section BU "The implementation folder"
  text
    These requirements are checked on the implementation folder, not through the driver.

req BU-001 "REGEN.json"
  text
    The implementation folder MUST contain `REGEN.json`, a JSON object with exactly the keys
    `lang` (`"ts"` or `"py"`), `build`, `test` and `driver`. Each of `build`, `test` and
    `driver` is a command string, or an object whose keys are Node.js `process.platform`
    values (such as `"win32"`) plus a required `"default"`, each mapping to a command string.
    Commands run in the implementation folder. `build` and `test` run through the platform
    shell; an empty `build` means there is nothing to build, and build output, if any, goes in
    `bin/`. `driver` is split on single spaces and started without a shell, so it MUST be plain
    space-separated words. The same REGEN.json MUST work on Windows and on Linux.
  static json "REGEN.json" matches {lang: "ts" | "py", build: command, test: command, driver: command}

req BU-002 "Own tests"
  text
    The implementation MUST have its own tests, including at least one for every MUST in this
    document, and the command `REGEN.json` names as `test` MUST pass.
  static command test exits 0 within 300

req BU-003 "Runtime and dependencies"
  text
    TypeScript MUST run on Node.js 22.18 or later directly by type stripping: erasable syntax
    only, no build step, relative imports with the `.ts` extension. Python MUST run on 3.11 or
    later. Only the language's standard library is used: nothing is installed, and a
    `package.json`, if there is one, declares no dependencies.
  static file "node_modules", "package-lock.json", "requirements.txt", "Pipfile", "poetry.lock" absent
  static text "package.json" not matching "\"(dev|peer|optional)?[dD]ependencies\""

req BU-004 "Size"
  text
    At most 800 non-blank lines of source in all: `.ts .mts .mjs .js` or `.py` files under the
    implementation folder, not counting tests (`*.test.*`, `*_test.*`, `test_*.py`, and
    anything under a `test/` or `tests/` folder).
  static lines "**/*.ts", "**/*.mts", "**/*.mjs", "**/*.js", "**/*.py" at most 800 excluding "**/*.test.*", "**/*_test.*", "**/test_*.py", "**/test/**", "**/tests/**"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
decision D-001 "The whole audit record is pinned, byte for byte"
  source extraction
  text
    The earlier implementation's main cross-language guarantee was byte-equal serialized
    audit records, including the human-readable `result_summary`. Pinning all of it makes the
    spec nearly as detailed as the code for summaries and number formatting. Decision: pin
    every audit member, including `result_summary`, number text, fixed-point text and
    canonical JSON (the three edges), and member presence rules; leave error message text,
    standard error and library shape open. Why: the audit record is what a downstream consumer
    stores and compares; if two implementations may differ in it, byte comparison stops being
    useful.
  rejected "Pin only the structured members and compare result_summary by pattern, as some earlier tests did: the earlier cross-language check compared whole records."

decision D-002 "Number text follows ECMAScript, not the host language"
  source extraction
  text
    Canonical JSON and summaries write numbers as text. The earlier implementation used
    JavaScript's `String(n)` and `toFixed`. Other languages differ: Python's `repr` writes
    `1e-07` and `1e+20` where JavaScript writes `1e-7` and `100000000000000000000`; Python's
    `'%.1f'` rounds exact ties to even (`20.25` → `20.2`) where `toFixed` rounds them up
    (`20.3`). Decision: the edges number-text/ecmascript and fixed-text/ecmascript. Why: byte
    equality across languages needs one rule; the earlier fixtures were produced with this one.
  rejected "Fixed significant digits everywhere: changes all existing expected bytes."

decision D-003 "The emitted contract version is 0.2.0"
  source extraction
  text
    Audit records carry `spec_version`, the version of the audit and behavior contract. The
    earlier implementation emitted `0.1.1`, and this spec changes the contract in places, so
    claiming `0.1.1` would be false. This is a different number from the document's own
    version, which tracks the wording. Decision: emit `"0.2.0"` (REQ-AU-001).
  rejected "Keep 0.1.1: dishonest about the changes."

decision D-007 "Non-finite inputs are written as strings in audits"
  source extraction
  text
    The earlier TypeScript wrote `NaN` inputs as the string `"NaN"` but let `Infinity`
    through, which its serializer then wrote as `null`; the other earlier implementation wrote
    `"Infinity"`. Decision: `"NaN"`, `"Infinity"`, `"-Infinity"` (REQ-AU-002); requests use
    the same three strings (REQ-IF-006), since JSON has no non-finite numbers. Why: symmetric,
    lossless, and matches one of the two earlier behaviors.
  rejected "null: loses which value it was."

decision D-009 "RH is clamped to [5, 100], not [5, 99]"
  source extraction
  text
    Stull's stated validity range is RH 5–99 %; the earlier input schema said implementations
    "MUST clamp to [5, 99]"; the earlier code clamped to [5, 100], and a test asserts
    `RH=120 → 100`. Decision: clamp to [5, 100] (REQ-WB-001); RH in (99, 100] computes without
    a marker. Why: code and tests agreed, and 100 % is a real humidity.
  rejected "Clamp to 99: contradicts the earlier tests."
  rejected "Mark RH above 99 as out of range: new behavior nobody asked for."

decision D-010 "Flag bands are in °F, and the °C path uses one exact expression"
  source extraction, primary source
  text
    MCO 6200.1E defines the bands in °F with inclusive lower bounds. The °C path converts
    first. Floating-point error can move a value across a boundary: for `26.66666666666666` °C,
    `(c*9)/5+32` gives `79.99999999999999` (white) but `c*1.8+32` gives `80` (green). Decision:
    REQ-FL-004 pins `(wetBulbC * 9) / 5 + 32`; REQ-WB-005 pins `((tempF - 32) * 5) / 9`. Why:
    the order of operations is observable at the boundaries.
  rejected "A tolerance at boundaries: makes flags ambiguous."

decision D-017 "canonical returns no audit"
  source extraction
  text
    Every computing operation returns a result and an audit. The canonical serializer is a
    utility, exposed so the suite can test number and string rules directly. Decision:
    `canonical` responds with `result` only (REQ-CJ-005). Why: an audit of the serializer would
    itself need serializing; nothing consumes it.
  rejected "A trivial audit: noise."

decision D-018 "Wet-bulb tolerance is ±1e-5 °C"
  source extraction
  text
    The imported wet-bulb fixtures carry a tolerance of 0.00001 and expected values to six
    decimals, generated by evaluating the formula in double precision. Stull's own claimed
    accuracy is about ±0.65 °C against observations; the tolerance here is about agreeing on
    the formula, not about meteorology. Why: any faithful double-precision evaluation passes; a
    wrong constant does not.
  rejected "A looser tolerance: would hide a mistyped constant."

decision D-019 "Malformed requests are errors, invalid values are results"
  source extraction
  text
    The earlier library returned `invalid_input` audits for non-finite numbers and unknown
    flag names, which are values a caller can legitimately pass. Missing fields and wrong JSON
    types have no library equivalent once a process boundary exists. Decision: missing fields
    and wrong JSON types are `bad_request`; non-finite numbers and unknown flag names are
    `invalid_input` results. Why: keeps the earlier invalid-input audits intact while giving
    the driver a clean failure for garbage.
  rejected "Turn everything into invalid_input audits: which field names would the audit carry for a missing member?"

decision D-021 "Object keys sort by UTF-16 code units"
  source extraction
  text
    The earlier serializers sorted keys with JavaScript's default comparison, which compares
    UTF-16 code units. That differs from code-point order only for keys mixing characters above
    U+FFFF with characters in U+E000–U+FFFF. All keys in audit records are ASCII, so it matters
    only for `canonical`. Decision: the edge json/sorted-utf16. Why: one rule, matching the
    earlier output.
  rejected "Code-point order."

decision D-024 "Error checks are a numbered order"
  source r02
  text
    The prose spec once said checks happen "in the order the table lists them" (the table
    listed `bad_request` first) and, in the same sentence, "`unknown_op` before input checks".
    The r02 builder chose the intended order and noted the wording was loose. Decision: the
    order is the numbered Errors list, declared once; `duramen check` rejects a requirement that
    restates it (T005).
  rejected "Keep a table and drop the first clause: still easy to misread."
